{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"5a55c3f396cb6085102801b66a1aae69e8b60386b34f2e48fafec2126d9e080d","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm preparing to dispatch four background agents, drafting a shared preamble that specifies the worktree path to use and warns them to avoid the other branch, and reminding them that since they'll be editing the same worktree concurrently, they should make targeted edits rather than rewriting shared files outright.\n\nI also need to spell out ground rules: no state-changing git commands since I handle commits myself, no dev servers or Electron launches, and verification steps using bun test, typecheck, and biome check. Then I'm outlining the first task — fixing the inefficient byte-array IPC transfer in App.tsx by sending the raw ArrayBuffer/Uint8Array instead of a JS number array, with corresponding updates to preload, main, storage, and type definitions.\n\nFor the thumbnail generation fix, I need to check all capture entry points (file picker, paste, drag/drop, clipboard, screenshot) for the same inefficient byte pattern, then make thumbnail generation non-blocking — save the item immediately and generate the thumbnail asynchronously in the background, using the existing job queue if possible, with a sensible fallback shown on the card meanwhile and recovery logic if the thumbnail is missing after restart.\n\nNext I'm looking at the asset protocol handler, which currently reads the whole file into memory — I want to switch it to streaming with range request support (206/416 responses, Accept-Ranges, Content-Length), while being careful about caching headers since Electron's custom protocol handling may not actually cache responses, and preserving the existing path containment security checks.\n\nFor video posters, local video cards currently load the full video file just to show a preview frame. I'm thinking about generating and caching a poster thumbnail at capture time (with backfill for existing items), using it for the card display while reserving full video loading for playback or detail view, likely via native thumbnail APIs on Windows/macOS with a fallback for Linux.\n\nI'm also noting that the current empty-dark-tile fallback for missing thumbnails should instead show type artwork, and flagging that the thumbnail/job work overlaps with other storage and AI-jobs changes, so coordination between those pieces is needed.\n\nFor the Archive tab, I want to shift loading of archived items to only happen when that tab is actually opened rather than every time Settings opens, refresh after restore/delete actions, and virtualize the archive grid similarly to the main library grid.\n\nOnly pure helper functions get unit tests, since there's no dev server for live preview. For effort levels, I'll leave it unspecified since the user didn't request it explicitly, using Sonnet for three of the tasks and Haiku for the archive one, then launch all four in one background message.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VisDFTd4YJQUU3f393iweN","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Capture bytes and background thumbnails","subagent_type":"general-purpose","model":"sonnet","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are fixing one performance problem in inkling, an Electron + React + TypeScript + Vite app with node:sqlite in the main process (Bun 1.4.2). Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root first. Its rules apply, especially \"capture is instant\": a card must appear before any slow processing finishes.\n\nWORKSPACE: C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\perf-library-io (branch perf/library-io). Work ONLY there. Never touch any other worktree or the live library. Three other agents are editing this same worktree right now:\n- one on the asset protocol handler in electron/main.mjs;\n- one on video posters (thumbnail/job code);\n- one on the Settings Archive tab in src/App.tsx.\nMake targeted edits with the Edit tool only. Never rewrite a shared file with Write: src/App.tsx, electron/main.mjs, electron/preload.cjs, src/electron.d.ts, src/lib/libraryApi.ts, electron/storage.mjs, electron/ai-jobs.mjs, electron/thumbnail.mjs. If an edit fails because the text moved, re-read and retry. Run NO git commands that change state (no add, commit, checkout, stash, reset or restore). The maintainer commits. Do not launch dev servers or Electron.\n\nTASK: file capture is slow in two ways.\n1. src/App.tsx (around line 2982) does `const bytes = Array.from(new Uint8Array(await file.arrayBuffer()));`. That sends a JS number array over Electron IPC, which is several times the size and a slow structured clone. Send the bytes as a Uint8Array or ArrayBuffer instead, end to end: renderer, src/lib/libraryApi.ts, electron/preload.cjs, src/electron.d.ts types, the main.mjs handler and storage. Grep for every capture path that sends file bytes and fix them all. Look for `Array.from(new Uint8Array`, `number[]` byte types, and `Buffer.from(` on IPC input. Paths to check: the Add/file picker, paste, drag and drop, clipboard image, screenshot and anything else. List them in your report.\n2. Image thumbnail generation (createImageThumbnail in electron/thumbnail.mjs) is awaited during capture before the item is returned, so the card waits on sharp. Make capture save the item and return at once, then generate the thumbnail in the background.\n   - Prefer the existing persisted job queue (electron/ai-jobs.mjs / storage) so an interrupted thumbnail recovers on restart. If that doesn't fit, use the smallest equivalent with restart recovery.\n   - When the thumbnail lands, the renderer must find out the same way other background item updates reach it.\n   - A thumbnail failure must keep the item, with no error card.\n   - Keep any cheap validation that rejects bad images (e.g. validateImageDimensions) where it must stay, but don't block on full resizing.\n   - Check what the card shows before the thumbnail exists, and make it sensible without loading huge originals into the grid if avoidable.\n\nTESTS: add focused bun tests for the new behavior: capture returns before the thumbnail exists, the background job produces it, failure keeps the item, and restart recovery works if you used the queue. Update existing tests that assumed a synchronous thumbnail. Run `bun test <files you touched>`, `bunx biome check <changed files>`, and `bun run typecheck`. Check the exit code with `echo $?`, not through a pipe. If typecheck errors are only in files another agent is editing, say so instead of fixing them.\n\nCode style: match the surrounding code. Comments only say why. No new dependencies and no network calls.\n\nREPORT (under 200 words): files changed, how bytes travel now, how and where thumbnails are generated and recovered, what the card shows meanwhile, tests run with results, and anything left unfinished."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W5BJdWdC7FXNtWgzpoypER","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Stream asset protocol with ranges","subagent_type":"general-purpose","model":"sonnet","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are fixing one performance problem in inkling, an Electron + React + TypeScript + Vite app with node:sqlite in the main process (Bun 1.4.2). Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root first. Its rules apply.\n\nWORKSPACE: C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\perf-library-io (branch perf/library-io). Work ONLY there. Never touch any other worktree or the live library. Three other agents are editing this same worktree right now:\n- one on capture IPC and background thumbnails;\n- one on video posters;\n- one on the Settings Archive tab.\nMake targeted edits with the Edit tool only. Never rewrite a shared file with Write, especially electron/main.mjs, which others are also editing. If an edit fails because the text moved, re-read and retry. Run NO git commands that change state (no add, commit, checkout, stash, reset or restore). The maintainer commits. Do not launch dev servers or Electron.\n\nTASK: the custom asset protocol handler in electron/main.mjs (around line 325, `const body = request.method === \"HEAD\" ? null : await readFile(realFile);`) reads the whole file into memory for every request. It also has no Range support, so a large local video or PDF is read fully before a byte is served, and video seeking can't work properly.\n- Stream the file instead (fs.createReadStream turned into a web stream via Readable.toWeb, or whatever works with Electron's protocol.handle Response).\n- Support single byte-range requests: 206 with Content-Range, and 416 with `Content-Range: bytes */size` for unsatisfiable ranges. Send Accept-Ranges: bytes and Content-Length. Keep HEAD working.\n- Keep every existing security check exactly as strict: realpath containment inside the assets directory, method checks and content types.\n- Caching: find out whether asset paths are immutable. Thumbnails may be regenerated at the same path. Only add cache headers that are correct and actually help for a custom protocol in Electron (ETag/Last-Modified from stat, for example). Don't add headers that do nothing, and say what you chose and why.\n- Move the request-to-response logic into a small module (e.g. electron/asset-response.mjs) so it can be tested without Electron, and keep main.mjs wiring thin.\n\nTESTS: add focused bun tests covering:\n- a full GET with the streamed body;\n- HEAD;\n- valid ranges, including open-ended `bytes=100-` and suffix `bytes=-100`;\n- an unsatisfiable range giving 416;\n- a malformed range giving a full 200;\n- path escape and symlink containment still rejected.\nRun `bun test <your test file>`, `bunx biome check <changed files>`, and `bun run typecheck`. Check the exit code with `echo $?`, not through a pipe. If typecheck errors are only in files another agent is editing, say so instead of fixing them.\n\nCode style: match the surrounding code. Comments only say why. No new dependencies.\n\nREPORT (under 200 words): files changed, the response behavior, the caching decision and why, tests run with results, and anything left unfinished."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0139YeE4p2jvqX4a9NqDXvm6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Background video posters for cards","subagent_type":"general-purpose","model":"sonnet","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are fixing one performance problem in inkling, an Electron + React + TypeScript + Vite app with node:sqlite in the main process (Bun 1.4.2). Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root first. Its rules apply, especially \"capture is instant\" and \"visual by type\".\n\nWORKSPACE: C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\perf-library-io (branch perf/library-io). Work ONLY there. Never touch any other worktree or the live library. Three other agents are editing this same worktree right now:\n- one on capture IPC plus moving IMAGE thumbnails to a background job (it touches electron/storage.mjs, ai-jobs.mjs, thumbnail.mjs, main.mjs and the capture code in App.tsx);\n- one on the asset protocol handler in main.mjs;\n- one on the Settings Archive tab in App.tsx.\nMake targeted edits with the Edit tool only. Never rewrite a shared file with Write. If an edit fails because the text moved, re-read and retry. If the image-thumbnail agent has added a background thumbnail job by the time you get there, reuse it for posters instead of building a parallel mechanism. Otherwise keep your additions self-contained so the two can be merged. Run NO git commands that change state (no add, commit, checkout, stash, reset or restore). The maintainer commits. Do not launch dev servers or Electron.\n\nTASK: local video file cards in the grid load the real video file just to show a frame. Look in src/components/ItemMedia.tsx and the card code in src/App.tsx for a <video> with preload or similar. Each card pulls a large file through the asset protocol.\n- Generate a poster image in the background for local video items and store it as the item's thumbnail asset, the way image thumbnails are stored.\n- Run it at capture, never blocking it. Also backfill existing local video items that have no poster, lazily or via the job queue, without a startup stall.\n- Electron's nativeImage.createThumbnailFromPath uses the Windows/macOS shell thumbnailer and needs no new dependency, so prefer it. On Linux, where it's unavailable, or on failure, leave the item without a poster. Do not add ffmpeg or any dependency.\n- The card should show the poster image. The video file loads only in the detail view or on play.\n- Without a poster, the card currently shows an empty dark tile with a tiny pill. It should show the shared video type artwork instead (see TypedArtwork in ItemMedia.tsx). Keep shared thumbnail logic in one place.\n- Video embed links (YouTube etc.) already have remote thumbnails. Leave them alone and do not add network calls.\n- Preview mode (`bun run dev`, sample data, no backend) must still render video cards sensibly.\n\nTESTS: add focused bun tests for the poster job logic:\n- a poster gets written and recorded;\n- failure keeps the item and doesn't retry forever;\n- backfill picks only local videos without posters.\nStub nativeImage. Run `bun test <files you touched>`, `bunx biome check <changed files>`, and `bun run typecheck`. Check the exit code with `echo $?`, not through a pipe. If typecheck errors are only in files another agent is editing, say so instead of fixing them.\n\nCode style: match the surrounding code. Comments only say why. No new dependencies.\n\nREPORT (under 200 words): files changed, how and when posters are generated and backfilled, what cards show with and without a poster, tests run with results, and anything left unfinished."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DpMEq4MM5BDP3p58Fup91u","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Lazy load and virtualize Archive tab","subagent_type":"general-purpose","model":"haiku","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are fixing one frontend performance problem in inkling, an Electron + React + TypeScript + Vite app (Bun 1.4.2). Read AGENTS.md at the worktree root first. Its rules apply.\n\nWORKSPACE: C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\perf-library-io (branch perf/library-io). Work ONLY there. Three other agents are editing this same worktree right now, and two of them touch src/App.tsx (capture code and video cards). Make targeted edits with the Edit tool only and NEVER rewrite src/App.tsx with Write. If an edit fails because the text moved, re-read and retry. Run NO git commands that change state (no add, commit, checkout, stash, reset or restore). The maintainer commits. Do not launch dev servers or Electron.\n\nTASK: Settings has an Archive tab. In src/App.tsx, find `loadArchivedItems`, near the Settings effect that loads the app version and library size. Every time Settings opens, it fetches every archived item (listArchivedItems) plus processing summaries for all of them (getProcessingSummaries), and the Archive tab renders every archived card at once with no virtualization.\n1. Load archived items only when the Archive tab is actually shown, not when Settings opens on another tab. Reload after a restore or permanent delete, the way it does now. Fetch processing summaries only if the archive UI actually uses them. If it does, fetch them only for what's rendered, or drop the call if it's unused.\n2. Virtualize the archive grid so only cards near the visible part of its scroll container render. First look at how the main library grid virtualizes in App.tsx (search for virtualization / GRID_GAP / TARGET_CARD_WIDTH / visible range logic) and reuse that approach or helper. Do not add a dependency.\n3. Keep everything else working:\n   - restore;\n   - permanent delete;\n   - any select-all or empty-archive actions;\n   - the empty state;\n   - keyboard focus;\n   - the current look of the Archive tab. Do not restyle it.\n\nIf you extract a pure helper (e.g. a visible-range calculation), add a small focused bun test for it. Don't write render-to-markup tests. Run `bunx biome check src/App.tsx <other changed files>`, `bun test <test files you touched>`, and `bun run typecheck`. Check the exit code with `echo $?`, not through a pipe. If typecheck errors are only in code another agent is editing (capture, video posters, electron/), say so instead of fixing them.\n\nREPORT (under 150 words): what changed, when the archive loads now, how virtualization works, whether processing summaries are still fetched, checks run with results, and anything left unfinished."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
