{"parentUuid":"98f9a2a1-736c-4e6f-b424-151e2cd2914f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a5d551ad-5e9e-431c-a603-b554797c9ccd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Zp4DYu3YskcWPhsbUAPkv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid-dom WebGPU liquid glass HTML-in-Canvas drawElement github\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"github.com%2FAndrewPrifer%2Fliquid dom\",\"url\":\"https://awesome.ecosyste.ms/projects/github.com%2FAndrewPrifer%2Fliquid-dom\"},{\"title\":\"Liquid DOM\",\"url\":\"https://docsearch.algolia.com/mcp/docs/repo/andrewprifer/liquid-dom\"},{\"title\":\"# Canvas UI puts WebGL shaders over real, interactive HTML\",\"url\":\"https://flaviocopes.com/canvas-ui.md\"},{\"title\":\"Liquid Glass for the Web\",\"url\":\"https://gittrend.io/repo/AndrewPrifer/liquid-dom\"},{\"title\":\"canvas ui\",\"url\":\"https://bestofjs.org/projects/canvas-ui\"},{\"title\":\"canvas ui webgl\",\"url\":\"https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/09/canvas-ui-webgl/\"},{\"title\":\"canvas ui\",\"url\":\"https://www.cssscript.com/canvas-ui/\"},{\"title\":\"canvas ui\",\"url\":\"https://flaviocopes.com/canvas-ui/\"},{\"title\":\"HTML-in-Canvas Lands: Introducing the First GPU‑Level UI Component Library\",\"url\":\"https://www.besthub.dev/articles/html-in-canvas-lands-introducing-the-first-gpu-level-ui-component-library-835f9da6ecc1\"}]\n\nThe search turned up two related projects, but no result ties them together directly.\n\n**liquid-dom** is a GitHub monorepo by Andrew Prifer for WebGPU liquid-glass rendering. Its `@liquid-dom/core` package provides an imperative DOM-backed scene graph, a WebGPU renderer, a reusable glass core, and layout classes. Other packages cover React, Three.js, and React Three Fiber. One listing reports about 1,860 stars. The docs describe a DOM-backed renderer that captures live HTML content into GPU textures for effects like blur and refraction. The results don't say whether it uses the `drawElement` or `drawElementImage` API specifically.\n\n**Canvas UI** is a separate open-source library built on the experimental HTML-in-Canvas API. Most components use Chrome's html-in-canvas API to lay out live DOM inside a canvas, capture it with drawElementImage, upload it as a texture and distort it in a shader. It has a WebGL build using GLSL and a WebGPU build using WGSL. Support is limited: the full experience needs Chrome with the canvas-draw-element flag, or an origin trial token, which runs from Chrome 148 through 150.\n\nThe search didn't return the liquid-dom repository's source or its exact API calls, so I'd check the repo directly to confirm whether it uses `drawElement` for capture.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"ff09776b-bcde-4898-a1c1-2a2439e4dbdc","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:08:40.847Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"liquid-dom WebGPU liquid glass HTML-in-Canvas drawElement github","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01W2P5LiiKqYFvf391Mo3kTc","content":[{"title":"github.com%2FAndrewPrifer%2Fliquid dom","url":"https://awesome.ecosyste.ms/projects/github.com%2FAndrewPrifer%2Fliquid-dom"},{"title":"Liquid DOM","url":"https://docsearch.algolia.com/mcp/docs/repo/andrewprifer/liquid-dom"},{"title":"# Canvas UI puts WebGL shaders over real, interactive HTML","url":"https://flaviocopes.com/canvas-ui.md"},{"title":"Liquid Glass for the Web","url":"https://gittrend.io/repo/AndrewPrifer/liquid-dom"},{"title":"canvas ui","url":"https://bestofjs.org/projects/canvas-ui"},{"title":"canvas ui webgl","url":"https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/09/canvas-ui-webgl/"},{"title":"canvas ui","url":"https://www.cssscript.com/canvas-ui/"},{"title":"canvas ui","url":"https://flaviocopes.com/canvas-ui/"},{"title":"HTML-in-Canvas Lands: Introducing the First GPU‑Level UI Component Library","url":"https://www.besthub.dev/articles/html-in-canvas-lands-introducing-the-first-gpu-level-ui-component-library-835f9da6ecc1"}]},"The search turned up two related projects, but no result ties them together directly.\n\n**liquid-dom** is a GitHub monorepo by Andrew Prifer for WebGPU liquid-glass rendering. Its `@liquid-dom/core` package provides an imperative DOM-backed scene graph, a WebGPU renderer, a reusable glass core, and layout classes. Other packages cover React, Three.js, and React Three Fiber. One listing reports about 1,860 stars. The docs describe a DOM-backed renderer that captures live HTML content into GPU textures for effects like blur and refraction. The results don't say whether it uses the `drawElement` or `drawElementImage` API specifically.\n\n**Canvas UI** is a separate open-source library built on the experimental HTML-in-Canvas API. Most components use Chrome's html-in-canvas API to lay out live DOM inside a canvas, capture it with drawElementImage, upload it as a texture and distort it in a shader. It has a WebGL build using GLSL and a WebGPU build using WGSL. Support is limited: the full experience needs Chrome with the canvas-draw-element flag, or an origin trial token, which runs from Chrome 148 through 150.\n\nThe search didn't return the liquid-dom repository's source or its exact API calls, so I'd check the repo directly to confirm whether it uses `drawElement` for capture."],"durationSeconds":6.371700500000036,"searchCount":1},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"98f9a2a1-736c-4e6f-b424-151e2cd2914f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
