{"parentUuid":"c3940da1-edb7-4aef-9850-2fb88a1c0ffd","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a5d551ad-5e9e-431c-a603-b554797c9ccd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DdLk16E9Tif1cKNpcuQ69y","type":"tool_result","content":"The page is GitHub's repository landing page, and the README is only partially rendered. It covers packages, install commands, and browser requirements, but not the rest of what you asked for.\n\n**Packages** (paraphrased from the README table)\n- `@liquid-dom/core`: imperative DOM-backed scene graph, WebGPU renderer, glass core, and layout classes.\n- `@liquid-dom/react`: React 19 bindings for layout and glass APIs.\n- `@liquid-dom/three`: adapter for compositing liquid glass over Three's WebGPU renderer.\n- `@liquid-dom/r3f`: React Three Fiber bridge built on the Three and React packages.\n- `@liquid-dom/layout`: renderer-agnostic layout engine with no renderer dependency.\n\n**Install** (verbatim from the README)\n\n```\npnpm add @liquid-dom/core\npnpm add @liquid-dom/react react react-dom\npnpm add @liquid-dom/three @liquid-dom/core three\npnpm add @liquid-dom/r3f @liquid-dom/react @react-three/fiber react react-dom three\npnpm add @liquid-dom/layout\n```\n\n**Browser and runtime requirements**\n- The liquid-glass renderer needs WebGPU. The README states, \"The liquid-glass renderer requires WebGPU.\"\n- DOM-backed `Html` content also needs the experimental HTML-in-Canvas API, currently available only behind Chrome's flag: `chrome://flags/#canvas-draw-element`.\n- The implementation uses `<canvas layoutsubtree>` and canvas paint events to copy live DOM content into GPU textures.\n- Rendering requires `navigator.gpu`. The DOM and React packages can build scene graphs in any browser-like environment.\n- Three integrations require Three's WebGPU renderer, not WebGLRenderer.\n- Reference: the WICG HTML-in-Canvas spec (https://wicg.github.io/html-in-canvas/).\n\n**Not in the provided content**\n- **`drawElementImage`:** not mentioned. The README refers to the canvas paint-event approach instead.\n- **React API:** component names and props are not given. The README only says `LiquidCanvas` owns the canvas, and that the React package wraps layout classes from `@liquid-dom/core/layout`.\n- **Rendering trigger** (rAF loop vs. on demand): not described.\n- **Fallbacks** for browsers without WebGPU or the flag: not described.\n- **Bundle size:** not listed.\n\n**License:** MIT. The page links to an \"MIT license\" and a LICENSE file.\n\nFor the API, rendering model, fallbacks, and bundle size, check the package READMEs (for example, `packages/react`) or the showcase site at liquid-dom-showcase.vercel.app."}]},"uuid":"ad2ea949-5321-4623-9434-125d22f09c9b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:08:53.072Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":307651,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page is GitHub's repository landing page, and the README is only partially rendered. It covers packages, install commands, and browser requirements, but not the rest of what you asked for.\n\n**Packages** (paraphrased from the README table)\n- `@liquid-dom/core`: imperative DOM-backed scene graph, WebGPU renderer, glass core, and layout classes.\n- `@liquid-dom/react`: React 19 bindings for layout and glass APIs.\n- `@liquid-dom/three`: adapter for compositing liquid glass over Three's WebGPU renderer.\n- `@liquid-dom/r3f`: React Three Fiber bridge built on the Three and React packages.\n- `@liquid-dom/layout`: renderer-agnostic layout engine with no renderer dependency.\n\n**Install** (verbatim from the README)\n\n```\npnpm add @liquid-dom/core\npnpm add @liquid-dom/react react react-dom\npnpm add @liquid-dom/three @liquid-dom/core three\npnpm add @liquid-dom/r3f @liquid-dom/react @react-three/fiber react react-dom three\npnpm add @liquid-dom/layout\n```\n\n**Browser and runtime requirements**\n- The liquid-glass renderer needs WebGPU. The README states, \"The liquid-glass renderer requires WebGPU.\"\n- DOM-backed `Html` content also needs the experimental HTML-in-Canvas API, currently available only behind Chrome's flag: `chrome://flags/#canvas-draw-element`.\n- The implementation uses `<canvas layoutsubtree>` and canvas paint events to copy live DOM content into GPU textures.\n- Rendering requires `navigator.gpu`. The DOM and React packages can build scene graphs in any browser-like environment.\n- Three integrations require Three's WebGPU renderer, not WebGLRenderer.\n- Reference: the WICG HTML-in-Canvas spec (https://wicg.github.io/html-in-canvas/).\n\n**Not in the provided content**\n- **`drawElementImage`:** not mentioned. The README refers to the canvas paint-event approach instead.\n- **React API:** component names and props are not given. The README only says `LiquidCanvas` owns the canvas, and that the React package wraps layout classes from `@liquid-dom/core/layout`.\n- **Rendering trigger** (rAF loop vs. on demand): not described.\n- **Fallbacks** for browsers without WebGPU or the flag: not described.\n- **Bundle size:** not listed.\n\n**License:** MIT. The page links to an \"MIT license\" and a LICENSE file.\n\nFor the API, rendering model, fallbacks, and bundle size, check the package READMEs (for example, `packages/react`) or the showcase site at liquid-dom-showcase.vercel.app.","durationMs":8931,"url":"https://github.com/AndrewPrifer/liquid-dom"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c3940da1-edb7-4aef-9850-2fb88a1c0ffd","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
