{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","id":"4bb92efa-d23e-46ca-8ba7-d09fa3d5a42d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:13:25.210Z","version":"2.1.305","querySource":"sdk","shapeHash":"a9ed8921dc21a185c51f032ab201ef86db193f2b4a3c27f0d2631dc37b2aa860","params":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"summarized"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"xhigh"}},"base":"4a1de851-6e7d-46ef-a02d-8f15184dd82a","keep":77,"tail":["31dd41217ca3ceee586bf1eb1b1f01c61de061aaa342cd060b33cd1a68d4b340","37e72554b38276aa7e1b9b797148b97f8fd0d0eb2e50dbc363c18c5ffbcdfe6e","d1dba323d734ed3ca5a10cb60c5380ed5c59f30d5087d3f57f8275643ac04905"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",79,"content",0],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"31d083b983e97cf77958505595273fb9a2e36f3735e9b93a343e73dc4edca346"}
{"parentUuid":"c71831eb-61d3-4ed1-b70c-138b03dc0680","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEjPJYagBqrQFsfJFXTE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the API confirms this machine has an Intel iGPU, which is actually ideal for testing performance on weaker hardware. Now I need to think through how to architecture the integration between liquid-dom's renderer model, which owns a canvas layout subtree, and this app's scene structure.\n\nThe tricky part is the toolbar's glass backdrop needs to refract the scrolling library grid beneath it, which means the entire grid region would need to live inside the canvas — risky because of potential text rendering differences from WebGPU capture, scroll performance concerns on the Intel iGPU, and possible added input latency from paint-based texture copying every frame.\n\nAnimated content in cards (GIFs, layout animations from GSAP) would force continuous repainting into the canvas, adding cost on top of what the CSS path already does. Since capturing content outside the canvas isn't possible, I'm considering whether an SVG displacement-based backdrop-filter could be a lighter alternative instead of pulling the whole grid into WebGPU.\n\nChromium does support reference filters as backdrop-filter values, which could give real-time refraction of the live scrolling backdrop without any canvas, flags, or WebGPU involvement, and it runs compositor-driven so no JS is needed on scroll. Performance seems reasonable for small elements, though I should keep in mind the user specifically referenced a paper prescribing the WebGPU liquid-dom approach with actual measurements, so I want to weigh both options carefully.\n\nSince the user explicitly directed me to follow that note as the implementation plan and accepted some performance tradeoff, I should honor it despite general guidance against over-engineering, since this was an explicit request. The scope though needs checking — the note originally scoped the glass effect only to floating chrome over the grid (search bar, zoom pill), while the user now wants it applied to all currently-translucent buttons, so I'm going through the existing `.glass` usages (search-field, view-controls, history-nav, query-chip, capture-error) to determine which actually qualify under the expanded request.\n\nThe edge-tab and card-video-badge are trickier: the badge sits inside the grid itself and would require per-card Glass nodes under virtualized scrolling, which is too complex for what's essentially a badge, so I'll keep it as CSS glass. The zoom pill in the expanded overlay, though, is explicitly called out in the note and needs its own LiquidCanvas wrapping the image stage, and I'm also noting the constraint that full-size 6000px JPEGs caused real hitches, so thumbnails should stay under glass while full images don't.\n\nBut then the actual full image sits outside the canvas, and the liquid canvas layer would draw over it anyway since scene-level Html backdrops get rendered — unless I zero out the Html's opacity while still letting it act as the backdrop source, which I'm not sure is even possible.\n\nRight, so the grid actually needs to live inside the canvas tree for refraction to work, since content outside the canvas can't be sampled for backdrop effects. So the structure should nest the Grid inside an Html backdrop at the scene level, with the glass container and its own Html content layered on top within the same LiquidCanvas/ZStack tree.\n\nThat means layout for the toolbar controls would need to be driven by liquid-dom's own layout engine rather than CSS, since anything positioned purely with the browser's layout wouldn't be visible to the canvas's capture mechanism. I'm weighing whether to instead keep an imperative core API approach — syncing glass node positions from measured DOM rects via ResizeObserver — but the control content itself still needs to be inside the canvas host, which brings back the same nesting requirement.\n\nActually a cleaner layering emerges: the canvas bottom layer holds the grid backdrop plus glass surfaces positioned over the toolbar control rects, with no Html children. Above that, a normal DOM layer holds the actual toolbar controls with transparent backgrounds, positioned exactly where the glass surfaces sit, so they paint normally on top rather than being refracted.\n\nPointer events should naturally resolve correctly since the toolbar controls sit above and intercept clicks, while the grid underneath catches events elsewhere. The tricky part is keeping the glass surfaces' positions synced with the DOM controls as they resize or animate—I'd need to measure rects continuously and re-render the glass each frame during transitions like the search field expanding. I should also check whether liquid-dom permits a Glass node with zero Html children, since this whole approach depends on treating glass as a pure visual surface decoupled from real content—essentially a sidecar overlay technique.\n\nThe more invasive alternative is making the grid's DOM scroller an actual child of the canvas element itself, which runs into complications with how liquid-dom's Renderer and Html components mount their own host elements—portaling React-rendered content into that host or relocating a React-owned node should still work since React tracks refs rather than DOM position.\n\nI should check liquid-dom's internals more closely: how the Renderer handles paint events, captures Html content into textures, and runs its frame loop.\n\nThinking about atlas texture sizing - a full-viewport backdrop capture at 1600x900 with DPR 2 gives a 3200x1800 texture, which should be manageable. I should also check the dist bundle's actual JS size versus the 1.2MB unpacked figure that includes source maps.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_read_input_tokens":108190,"output_tokens":4650,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4385},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4650,"cache_read_input_tokens":108190,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":48049,"thinkingDisplay":"summarized","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgEjPJEjGFebehLXPigRk","requestRef":"4bb92efa-d23e-46ca-8ba7-d09fa3d5a42d","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"74c7a6b7-e5fc-4d24-b3bd-9de8782097cb","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:14:14.139Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
