{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"a4f85a861af2e1240924cda7c91ad3ab3322c2a90f6d96f962fa4d589519fbaa","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm realizing every pass per container is running at full canvas resolution rather than being scissored to the glass element's actual bounds—the composite, blur chains, displacement field, and shadow mask are all unnecessarily processing the whole screen each frame.\n\nRendering only needs to happen when something changes — scroll or hover — and each frame requires capturing the grid texture plus a handful of fullscreen passes, which should stay within budget at 60-75Hz on this iGPU, consistent with the note's measured 13.3ms frames at 75Hz.\n\nI'm also considering a hybrid approach where the canvas isn't the grid's actual display layer but just a lens overlay per control, letting Chromium handle normal grid scrolling for free while a small lens canvas copies the backdrop from the grid underneath each glass element.\n\nBut that backdrop copy still requires capturing the grid itself, since elements must be direct children of the canvas per the HTML-in-Canvas spec — there's no way around having the grid live inside the canvas. So the full approach with the grid rendered inside the canvas, as the note's author designed and measured, is the right one. I also need to check whether the edge tab, which sits outside `<main>`, has its glass rect partly outside the canvas bounds based on its CSS positioning.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VSA6eKAhxkzNNKCTAwFhn3","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && sed -n 735,800p src/App.css && grep -n \"card-video-badge\\b\\|\\.card-video-badge {\" src/App.css | head -3 && grep -n \"GradualBlur\" src/App.tsx | head && sed -n 1640,1700p src/App.tsx","description":"Read edge tab CSS and GradualBlur usage"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
