 infinite;\nsrc/App.css:2252:  animation: spin 1.2s linear infinite;\nsrc/App.css:2514:  animation: spin 1.2s linear infinite;\nsrc/App.css:3303:  animation: spin 1.2s linear infinite;\nsrc/App.css:4478:  animation: memory-caret 1s steps(1) infinite;\nsrc/App.css:4758:  animation: memory-caret 1s steps(1) infinite;\nsrc/App.css:5163:  animation: mascot-bob 0.9s ease-in-out infinite;\nsrc/App.tsx:933:        <video\nsrc/components/ItemMedia.tsx:17:import { autoplayEmbedUrl, providerLabel } from \"../lib/ingestion/video-links\";\nsrc/components/ItemMedia.tsx:228:  \"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\";\nsrc/components/ItemMedia.tsx:242:            src={autoplayEmbedUrl(item.video.embedUrl)}\nsrc/components/ItemMedia.tsx:270:        <video className=\"detail-native-video\" src={item.fileUrl} controls preload=\"metadata\" />","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b228a61a-a8c2-4e07-b150-38556b1799bd","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"parentUuid":"de1f37f4-fba3-43ff-abc6-50353dce397c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14812247 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1963aabf-8729-4c91-a6d7-e9386d94237b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:20:39.030Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14812247 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"766c86159b280d94bee2a2066735dea7c8bf633a75a00357ec327597ca6d2891","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm mapping out the adapter API design, noting how the content source handles paint events and syncing while the glass core handles rendering layers to an output texture. I'm now wondering whether that output texture can directly be the canvas's own texture.\n\nSince drawFrame ends with a blit from the composer's current texture to the output texture via a render pass, the canvas texture needs RENDER_ATTACHMENT usage (which it has by default), so yes, this should work directly without needing frame preservation logic since we re-render immediately on resize.\n\nI'm also considering ownership of the device for lifecycle handling, and thinking through how React and imperative DOM can coexist in the same canvas: React renders no children into the canvas itself, while content-source hosts are appended imperatively, with portal elements going through the Html node's host option. Now I'm checking whether React 19 passes through the custom `layoutsubtree` attribute as a lowercase DOM attribute.\n\nI'm sketching the overall component flow: the Stage component renders a wrapper div, conditionally including a canvas when in liquid mode, alongside a portal for its children into a detached content div. On mount, it appends that content div to the wrapper for CSS mode and kicks off an async capability check to decide whether to switch into liquid mode, and once in liquid mode with the canvas mounted, it builds the scene by wiring the content div into an Html node via setElement.\n\nI'm working through the timing concern: moving the content element into the host happens when the Html node is created, but the host only gets appended into the canvas once sync runs, so calling sync right after scene creation avoids any visible gap. The actual first render depends on the browser's paint event firing so textures can be copied from the painted content, and if that render happens synchronously within the paint handler, the canvas update should land in the same frame without a blank flash.\n\nI'm also mapping out the render pipeline wiring: registering the paint handler to call render, setting up a ResizeObserver to resize the canvas and trigger a requestPaint so the backdrop texture gets recaptured at the new size, and handling target rect updates (from resize/mutation/transition) by syncing glass nodes and re-rendering without needing a fresh backdrop capture, while making sure the content source's sync is called whenever the scene itself changes.\n\nNow I'm working through how to locate glass targets, since they can live both inside the main content area (like a capture bar) and outside it (like an edge tab), which means the selector logic needs to look across multiple roots rather than assuming a single container.\n\nI'm also considering the cost of a MutationObserver watching the whole app shell — since virtualized card scrolling generates frequent mutation records, I want to avoid requerying the entire DOM tree on every change and instead batch updates with a dirty flag checked on the next animation frame to keep things cheap.\n\nInstead of watching the full app shell, I'm narrowing the observed roots to just the capture bar and edge tab elements, including the edge tab itself as a target when it carries the glass class. For the zoom pill, I'm planning to extract a shared non-React \"LiquidGlassScene\" class that manages the device, core, source, canvas, and scene, exposing methods to set the backdrop, size, and shapes, then wrap it with two thin React components for the lens and pill.\n\nActually, I'm settling on one unified `LiquidGlass` component with props for class name, portaled backdrop children, an array of root refs to scan for `.glass` targets, and an optional ready-state callback. The pill version just passes its own ref as the target root so the lens picks up the pill's glass styling.\n\nFor distinguishing the lens case in CSS, I'm thinking of marking lens targets with a different `data-liquid` value so the stylesheet keeps the pill's outer shadow while stripping the background/blur/border that the canvas-rendered liquid effect replaces. I'm also planning a shared module-level capability/device detection helper.\n\nA single GPU device can back both the library stage and the lens stage, each with its own core and canvas context, and since Dawn deduplicates identical pipeline and shader module objects by content hash, recreating a pipeline on the same device when the lens opens should be cheap rather than requiring a fresh compile.\n\nI also need to handle device-loss recovery by reverting to CSS mode — restoring contentEl to its wrapper, removing the canvas, clearing the data-liquid attribute — plus logging uncaptured device errors, and defining the error path for when copying the element's image into a texture fails.\n\nFeature detection should check both `copyElementImageToTexture` on GPUQueue and `layoutSubtree` on HTMLCanvasElement to confirm support. For Electron, I'll enable the CanvasDrawElement blink feature per-window via `webPreferences.enableBlinkFeatures` rather than touching the global command line, since nothing else currently appends that switch in main. Testing with a plain browser preview should show the CSS fallback kicking in correctly when the flag isn't set, confirming the app works across both modes.\n\nNow checking the \"no continuously repainting animations\" rule against our on-demand rendering — hover transitions in the grid trigger paint events but only on interaction, which is fine, and the mascot lives outside the canvas in the sidebar so it's unaffected. I should grep for any `infinite` CSS animations that might run inside cards, like a processing shimmer effect, since those would violate the rule if they're inside the canvas area.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0112U1p6Ys42GjhrPCHaYb85","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && grep -n \"infinite\" src/App.css src/*.css | head -20; grep -n \"autoPlay\\|autoplay\\|<video\" src/App.tsx src/components/ItemMedia.tsx | head","description":"Find continuous animations that could live in the grid"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
