{"parentUuid":"204d3a3f-647d-44b9-b9fc-a58fb7699d22","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14792817 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"13c0ed34-3dd2-475a-8226-c663b1137c09","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:22:46.066Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14792817 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"C:\\Users\\chris\\.t3\\worktrees\\inkling\\t3-47ff5304","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","version":"2.1.305","gitBranch":"feat/glass-surface","slug":"purrfect-munching-gadget"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"fad76192-bf94-45bb-9f1a-d29cda113db7","hash":"87e342604fa03aab2979c688552626530d478053c7a105ecbd34093483f9b03a","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm working out how to position the lens's replica image declaratively—figuring out the image's center relative to the frame (accounting for offset) and its displayed size based on the zoom-to-fit ratio.\n\nI should match the lens host background to the item-stage background, and skip rendering the pixel grid overlay and anything during the \"is-flying\" state since the pill is hidden then. Now I'm sketching the LiquidGlass component itself, setting up refs for the wrapper and canvas plus a content div that holds CSS-mode children while a GPU context gets initialized.\n\nStill writing component logic...\n\nI need content to fill the host absolutely, holding either library-scroll content or the lens replica image. Now I'm sketching out the LiquidSurface class structure that manages the scene, backdrop, container, and glass instances tied to each element.\n\nSetting up WebGPU context and content source...\n\nI'm double-checking the ordering issue: render must fire after the content source copies changed hosts, since calling the texture copy outside a paint event seems to be permitted as long as a cached snapshot exists from the last render — liquid-dom does this same pattern via queueMicrotask after mutations, so this should be safe.\n\nTo avoid redundant renders per frame, I want to coalesce them: paint events trigger immediate render for same-frame sync, while rect changes just mark dirty and call requestPaint() to schedule a paint event next frame. Since rAF runs before paint in the frame lifecycle, calling requestPaint() there still lets the paint event fire within the same frame, keeping everything funneled through a single render path.\n\nFor target tracking, I'm setting up a syncShapes function that reads target rects relative to the canvas and updates the Glass nodes, calling requestPaint() if anything changed. I need ResizeObservers on the wrapper and targets, a MutationObserver watching for class/style/attribute changes to schedule syncs via rAF, and listeners for transition/animation start events that run a rAF loop until rects stabilize across consecutive frames.\n\nRather than exactly counting active animations (which can drift if elements get removed mid-transition), a simpler and more robust approach is to just set a timed window on any start event -- loopUntil = now + 600ms, since the longest transitions here are around 320ms -- and run the sync loop until that deadline, with one extra sync on end events.\n\nI'm also realizing the sidebar's width animation combined with the main content's padding transition means the wrapper resizes every frame, which triggers the ResizeObserver repeatedly and could force canvas reconfiguration and render target recreation during the whole animation -- a potential performance hitch I need to check against how the sidebar actually resizes.\n\nFor the glass shape computation, I'm working out how to derive each target's rectangle relative to the canvas, parse the corner radius from computed styles (handling full-pill 999px cases by capping at half width/height), and detect asymmetric border-radius cases like the edge tab's one-sided rounding, extending the glass shape outward on the flat side to compensate.\n\nI also need to handle hidden or invisible targets properly -- elements with display:none should be skipped entirely since their rect collapses to zero, but opacity:0 elements are trickier since the box still exists geometrically and could wrongly render a glass shape even though it's invisible, so I need to check computed opacity and visibility per element, though this gets complicated when opacity is set on a parent container rather than the glass node itself.\n\nNow I'm restructuring the main layout so the library-scroll section gets wrapped inside a LiquidGlass container, setting up the stage element's positioning...\n\nSince main has overflow hidden and border-radius on desktop, the canvas inherits that clipping automatically. I need the content layer's background to fall back to the library-frame color on desktop so uncovered areas blend correctly, while mobile needs different handling since main lacks that frame background there.\n\nActually since the content element inherits through the DOM chain regardless of liquid or CSS mode, I can just use a CSS variable set on main (`--stage-background`) that defaults to `var(--paper)` and switches to `var(--library-frame)` in the desktop media query, then have the content simply reference that variable—letting inheritance handle both modes cleanly. Now I'm positioning the library-scroll element absolutely within the content container, aligning it to the top padding...\n\nThat matches the comment about the first card sitting 100px below the toolbar's top. Now I'm checking whether `.library-header` has any height that would throw off my flow calculation, since library-scroll being pulled out of flow depends on what precedes it being empty — I should verify that section's CSS to confirm it's truly collapsed.\n\nThe transition overlay inside library-scroll stays absolute inset within the canvas, so that's fine. I'm now considering the edge fade component sitting above the canvas with its own z-index and blur effect, and whether it could be folded into the canvas in liquid mode — probably better to leave it separate for now.\n\nI also realize the backdrop-filter blur over the canvas means it reruns on every canvas update, same as before, so no new performance regression there. Checking z-ordering across the header, capture bar, modals, stage, and edge fade confirms the stage sits correctly as an absolutely positioned layer where library-scroll used to be in the DOM.\n\nNow I need to convert the .library-scroll styles to absolute positioning and check other rules that reference it, plus verify the mobile layout padding on the header still works correctly.\n\nFor mobile, the library-header is empty so its height just comes from the 30px padding-top, and I should check Electron's minWidth setting since mobile layout only really applies when previewing in a narrow browser window. I'll fold that header offset into a mobile-specific padding variable before writing the actual CSS.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The zoom pill will use a pill-shaped lens with a thumbnail of the image, positioned based on the actual image location. Before adjusting the layout, I'm reviewing all rules tied to the library scroller's position.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kbqx4ra5568ii3sh7Gh2as","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/chris/.t3/worktrees/inkling/t3-47ff5304 && grep -n \"library-header\\|titlebar-height:\\|\\.library-scroll\" src/App.css | head -20; grep -n \"minWidth\\|minHeight\" electron/main.mjs | head; sed -n 1995,2010p src/App.css; grep -n \"\\.item-zoom-pill {\" -A 22 src/App.css; grep -n \"^\\.item-stage {\\|^\\.item-stage-zoom {\" -A 14 src/App.css","description":"Check header, titlebar and item stage CSS"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
